<p><b>Project Title:</b> Sankofa Knowledge Systems: Bridging African Diaspora Health Wisdom with AI-Powered Community Navigation to Advance U.S. Health Equity</p>
<p><b>Proposal I.D.:</b> 164054</p>
<p><b>Applicant Name:</b> Anika Amie</p>
<p><b>Legal Name of Applicant Organization:</b> The Collaborative Advocate Foundation</p>

<h2>Brief Proposal Narrative</h2>

<h3>The Crisis in Health Knowledge Systems</h3>

<p>The U.S. health knowledge system was not built for the communities most affected by health inequities. In Austin, Texas, Black and Latino residents face widening disparities driven not only by structural barriers to care, but by a knowledge system that excludes their lived experience, cultural health practices, and community-generated evidence from health decision-making. Census ACS, CDC PLACES, and EPA EJScreen data for Austin ZIP codes 78702, 78721, 78741, and 78744 reveal uninsurance rates 3x the county average, food desert designation, and chronic disease prevalence 40-60% above state baselines.</p>

<p>Globally, this problem has been addressed. Ghana's CHPS program integrates traditional healers into primary care, reducing maternal mortality by 40%. Brazil's Family Health Strategy trains 260,000+ community health agents bridging biomedical and Afro-Brazilian healing practices. Jamaica's community health aides preserve Caribbean bush medicine alongside Western clinical care. These nations show that braiding ancestral wisdom with biomedical science makes health systems more effective and equitable.</p>

<h3>Our Vision: Sankofa Knowledge Systems</h3>

<p>"Sankofa" -- an Akan concept from Ghana meaning "go back and get it" -- captures our vision. TCAF proposes to retrieve the health knowledge, healing practices, and community evidence systems the U.S. has excluded, and bring them forward into an AI-powered knowledge system serving communities who need it most.</p>

<h3>Three Pillars of Action</h3>

<p><b>Pillar 1: Knowledge Generation and Preservation (Months 1-18).</b> Community-led participatory research in Austin's Eastern Crescent neighborhoods will document diaspora health knowledge through oral histories, healing practice inventories, and community narratives. Working with elders, traditional practitioners, and CHWs, we will create a digital archive accessible through our Sankofa Health Network. International partners in Ghana, Brazil, and Jamaica will conduct parallel documentation, building a cross-cultural knowledge repository.</p>

<p><b>Pillar 2: Knowledge System Integration (Months 6-30).</b> We will deploy technology that braids traditional, community, and biomedical knowledge into a unified navigation system. TCAF's 24-platform ecosystem -- covering health (HerHealth Network, TheHealthyBlkMan), education (ThriveUp Academy), housing (LifeBridge), workforce (Mission Transition), and cognitive health (SafeCogniCare) -- provides the foundation. Our AI engines will incorporate diaspora health knowledge alongside clinical evidence, enabling CHWs to navigate residents to both biomedical and culturally grounded resources.</p>

<p><b>Pillar 3: Bidirectional Knowledge Exchange (Months 12-36).</b> Austin-based CHWs will travel to partner sites in Accra, Salvador, and Kingston to learn ancestral health integration firsthand, while international counterparts visit Austin to learn technology-enabled navigation. This produces co-created curricula, jointly authored publications, and a replicable framework.</p>

<h3>Existing Infrastructure</h3>

<p>TCAF is not starting from zero. Our 24-platform ecosystem is deployed and serving communities: <b>HerHealth Network</b> navigates 70+ women's health conditions with validated screenings (PHQ-9, GAD-7, C-SSRS); <b>Whole-Person Health</b> delivers mental wellness with six clinical instruments; <b>RPLICE</b> integrates SDOH data by ZIP code (Census ACS, CDC PLACES, EPA EJScreen, SVI); <b>MentorConnect</b> links families to 30+ programs; and <b>Mission Transition</b> serves veterans. Four AI engines power the system: RAG knowledge base, Bidirectional Planning, SDOH Analytics, and Community Intelligence. RWJF funding goes to knowledge generation and partnership -- not platform construction.</p>

<h3>Leadership</h3>

<p><b>Anika Amie, Founder and Executive Director</b> -- A Black woman community health advocate who built TCAF's ecosystem from lived experience navigating systemic barriers. The team includes CHWs rooted in Austin's Eastern Crescent, academic advisors, technology staff, and international partners with decades of experience integrating traditional and biomedical knowledge systems.</p>

<h3>Expected Outcomes</h3>

<p>1) Digital archive of 500+ diaspora health knowledge entries, publicly accessible and AI-indexed; 2) Sankofa Health Network with culturally braided knowledge serving 2,000+ members annually; 3) 20 CHWs trained via Bidirectional Knowledge Exchange curriculum; 4) 3 peer-reviewed publications co-authored with international partners; 5) Open-source "Culturally Braided Health Knowledge Framework" toolkit; 6) National Community of Practice with 50+ organizations.</p>

<h3>Dissemination</h3>

<p>Results disseminated through: peer-reviewed publications (AJPH, Health Affairs, Global Health Promotion); conference presentations (APHA, AcademyHealth); open-source toolkit shared through the Community of Practice; documentary series; and policy briefs for state and local health departments and funders.</p>

<h3>Why TCAF, Why Now</h3>

<p>TCAF has already built the technology infrastructure most health equity organizations aspire to. What we have not yet done is systematically integrate the ancestral and cultural health knowledge of the communities we serve. This project fills that gap. As public health institutions face political threats, community-controlled knowledge systems become essential resilience infrastructure. We create a system communities own, that reflects their wisdom, and that cannot be dismantled.</p>

